@node Opening Esploro
@chapter Opening Esploro

@table @asis
@item @kbd{Super+e}
The Esploro window on the workspace you're on, or a new one there.  On
Vikix this is the key for files; where Esploro isn't installed, it
opens PCManFM instead.

@item @kbd{Super+Alt+Shift+e}
A new Esploro window, even where one is open.

@item @code{esploro @var{folder}}
From a shell, the launcher, or a program opening a folder: @var{folder}
in the Esploro window on your workspace, or a new one there.
@code{esploro --new @var{folder}} always opens a new window.

@item @kbd{M-x esploro}
From Emacs, once Esploro has been opened (or with @file{emacs/} on
Emacs's @code{load-path}).  With a prefix argument, a new window.
@end table

Esploro's window is a frame of the Emacs that is running, which on
Vikix is a daemon started at login, so it opens at once.  The first
time, the command loads the window's code into that Emacs; nothing in
your Emacs configuration is needed.

To close it, use File, Close Esploro, @kbd{C-x C-c}, or close the window
(@kbd{Super+q} on Vikix).  Only that window closes: Emacs, and its other
frames, go on.

@node The window
@chapter The window

@table @asis
@item The list
The folder's files, folders first, with their size and when they last
changed.  The line at the top says where you are, how the list is
sorted, and when hidden files are shown or a filter is on.

@item Places
Down the left side: your home, the usual folders (Documents, Downloads,
Pictures, Music, Videos, Projects, Dropbox, when they exist), drives
plugged in (in @file{/run/media}), the bookmarks GTK programs keep
(@file{~/.config/gtk-3.0/bookmarks}), and the Trash.  Click one to go
there.  @kbd{F9}, or View, Places, hides them or shows them again.

@item The tool bar
Back, Forward, Up, Home, Places, Two Panes, Preview, New Folder, Copy,
Cut, Paste, Trash, Undo, Find, Refresh and Help.

@item The menus
File, Edit, View, Go and Help.  Esploro's frame shows only these; your
other Emacs frames keep Emacs's own menus.
@end table

@node Selecting and opening
@chapter Selecting and opening

@table @asis
@item Click
Selects the file: the one the next command acts on.

@item @kbd{Ctrl}+click
Adds the file to the selection, or takes it out.

@item @kbd{Shift}+click
Selects every file from the one selected last to the one clicked.

@item Double-click, or @kbd{RET}
Opens it.  A folder opens in the same window.  A file that a window
already has open goes to that window rather than opening a second time.
Text opens in Emacs, in one of its other frames.  Anything else opens
in its usual program (as @file{~/.config/mimeapps.list} says).

@item Right-click
What can be done with the file under the mouse, or, on empty space,
with the folder.

@item File, Open With
Opens the selection with a program you name.
@end table

Edit, Select All, Select None and Invert Selection work on every file
in the folder.  Dired's keys still mark: @kbd{m} marks, @kbd{u}
unmarks, @kbd{U} unmarks all.

@node Changing files
@chapter Changing files

@table @asis
@item Copy, Cut, Paste (@kbd{M-w}, @kbd{C-w}, @kbd{C-y})
Copy or cut the selection, go to another folder, and paste.  Pasting
where the files already are makes copies beside them (``notes
copy.txt'').  Other file managers can paste what Esploro copied, and
Esploro can paste what they copied.

@item Duplicate
A copy of each selected file beside it.

@item Copy To, Move To
Copies or moves the selection into a folder you type.

@item Rename (@kbd{F2})
A new name for the file selected.

@item New Folder (@kbd{+})
A folder in the one you're in.

@item Move to Trash (@kbd{Delete})
Puts the selection in the Trash.  Nothing is deleted for good there.

@item Undo (@kbd{C-/})
Puts back the last change Esploro made.  Undo again for the one before.
Undoing a copy puts the copy in the Trash, rather than deleting it.
@end table

Every change is a plan, a short list of steps, checked whole before
anything happens: a file that would be overwritten, or a folder that
isn't there, stops it with nothing changed, and the message says why.
Changes run in the background, so a long copy doesn't stop Emacs.  Each
one is kept in a journal (@file{~/.local/state/esploro/journal/}),
which is what undo reads.

Dired's own keys (@kbd{C}, @kbd{R}, @kbd{D} and the rest) still work
in the list, but around the journal: what they do can't be undone from
Esploro.

@node Drag and drop
@chapter Drag and drop

@table @asis
@item In
Drag files from another program onto the list: they're copied into the
folder (moved, when the other program says so, often with @kbd{Shift}).
Like every change, it can be undone.  Files dropped on the folder
they're already in are left alone.

@item Out
Press on a file anywhere on its row and drag it to another program: a
browser's upload, a mail, a terminal (which types its name).  It's
copied; hold @kbd{Shift} to move it.  Selected files go together.

@item Between panes
Drag from one pane to the other: the file is copied into that pane's
folder.
@end table

@node The preview
@chapter The preview

Down the right of the window, the file under the cursor:

@table @asis
@item A picture
The picture, scaled to fit.
@item A PDF, a video
The PDF's first page, or a frame of the video.  Esploro makes it once,
in the background (``making a preview...'' while it does), and keeps it
in @file{~/.cache/esploro}, so the next look is at once.
@item Text, code
Its first lines, in the colours its mode gives them.
@item A folder
How many things are in it, and their names.
@item Anything else
What it is, as @command{file} says.
@item Several selected
How many, their size together, and their names.
@end table

Below each: its size, when it last changed, and the windows that have
it open.  The preview follows the cursor and the mouse once they rest,
so holding an arrow key down stays quick.  With two panes, it shows the
pane used last; each Esploro window has its own.

@kbd{F11} (View, Preview, or the tool bar) hides it, or shows it again.
Hidden stays hidden, in every Esploro window, until you turn it on.

@node Two panes
@chapter Two panes

@kbd{F3} (View, Two Panes, or the tool bar) shows two folders side by
side.  The new pane starts at the same folder; each then has its own
folder, history, sort and hidden files.  @kbd{F3} again goes back to
one.

@table @asis
@item Copy to Other Pane (@kbd{C-c C-c})
@itemx Move to Other Pane (@kbd{F6})
The selection, into the other pane's folder.  Both are on the Edit
menu and the right-click menu.
@end table

The places and the tool bar act on the pane used last.

@node Windows and workspaces
@chapter Windows and workspaces

Each workspace can have its own Esploro window, at its own folder.
@kbd{Super+e} goes to the one on the workspace you're on, or opens one
there; one on another workspace stays where it is.  To have two on the
same workspace, use @kbd{Super+Alt+Shift+e}, File, New Window
(@kbd{C-x 5 2}), or @code{esploro --new}.

Esploro knows which window is on which workspace by asking StumpWM.
Without StumpWM, @code{esploro @var{folder}} uses any Esploro window.

@node Looking around
@chapter Looking around

@table @asis
@item Sorting (View, or @kbd{s})
By name, size (largest first), time (newest first) or kind.  The same
again, or View, The Other Way Round, turns it round.  @kbd{s} goes
through them in turn.

@item Hidden files (@kbd{.})
Shows or hides the files whose names start with a dot.

@item Filter (@kbd{/})
Shows only the names holding some text, in any case, until you refresh
(@kbd{F5}).

@item Find Below (@kbd{M-s f})
Every file below this folder whose name matches, as a list you can
open, copy and drag from like any folder.  Back returns.

@item Going (@kbd{M-Left}, @kbd{M-Right}, @kbd{M-Up}, @kbd{C-l})
Back, forward, the folder above, and a folder typed, with completion.
@end table

@node The Trash
@chapter The Trash

Esploro uses the Trash other programs use (the freedesktop one, in
@file{~/.local/share/Trash}).  Go, The Trash (or Trash in the places)
shows what's in it.  There, Restore from the Trash puts the selection
back where it was; undo puts it in the Trash again.  Empty the Trash
deletes everything in it, for good, after asking: that one can't be
undone.

@node Where files are open
@chapter Where files are open

Beside a file that a window has open, Esploro says which: ``open in
Emacs on 2 (unsaved)'', ``open in mpv on 4''.  It asks StumpWM for the
windows and their programs, @file{/proc} for what those hold open or
were started on, and Emacs for its buffers.  Opening such a file goes to
its window instead of opening it again.

