@node The command line
@chapter The command line

@table @code
@item esploro [@var{folder}]
@var{folder} (by default the one you're in) in the Esploro window on
your workspace, or a new one there.
@item esploro --new [@var{folder}]
A new Esploro window.
@item esploro --where [@var{path}@dots{}]
Which windows have @var{path} open, or every file that's open.
@end table

What the window uses (each answers with one s-expression):

@table @code
@item esploro apply [@var{file}]
Applies the plan in @var{file}, or on standard input: one step a line,
@code{(:copy "/from" "/to")}, @code{(:move @dots{})},
@code{(:rename "/path" "new name")}, @code{(:mkdir "/path")},
@code{(:trash "/path")}.  Anything else is refused.
@item esploro undo
Undoes the last applied plan.
@item esploro where @var{folder}
The files in @var{folder} a window has open.
@item esploro open @var{path}
Goes to the window that has @var{path}, or opens it.
@item esploro preview @var{path}
A PNG of @var{path} (a picture, a PDF or a video), made once and kept.
@item esploro trash-list
@itemx esploro restore @var{name}@dots{}
@itemx esploro empty-trash
The Trash.
@end table

@node Writing commands
@chapter Writing commands

The core defines commands once, for the kinds of file they're for:

@lisp
(esploro:define-file-command shrink ((path :image) :changes t)
  "Plan a smaller copy beside it."
  (list (list :copy path (esploro::free-name path " small"))))
@end lisp

Kinds: @code{:folder}, @code{:image}, @code{:video}, @code{:audio},
@code{:pdf}, @code{:text}, @code{:lisp}, @code{:archive}, @code{:file}
(anything but a folder) and @code{t} (anything).  A command with
@code{:changes t} returns plan steps instead of touching files, so it
is checked, journaled and undoable like the rest.  Offering these in
the window's menus is the next step.

@bye
